{"step_index":10,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T07:48:16Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-09T13:18:16+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-09T13:18:16+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/college/College%20sem%205/bie%20artifacts/BIE_complete_answer_bank.md`\nTotal Lines: 1051\nTotal Bytes: 59394\nShowing lines 801 to 1051\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n801: \n802: DMAIC is the core methodology of Six Sigma for improving existing processes. It stands for:\n803: \n804: **1. D — Define:**\n805: - Define the problem, project goals, and customer requirements (CTQ — Critical to Quality).\n806: - Identify the process to be improved.\n807: - Create a project charter with scope, objectives, team members, and timeline.\n808: \n809: **2. M — Measure:**\n810: - Measure the current performance of the process.\n811: - Collect data on defects, cycle time, and key process variables.\n812: - Establish the baseline sigma level.\n813: - Tools: Data collection, check sheets, Pareto chart.\n814: \n815: **3. A — Analyze:**\n816: - Analyze the data to identify root causes of defects and process variation.\n817: - Find the gap between current performance and desired performance.\n818: - Tools: Cause-and-effect diagram (Fishbone), 5-Why analysis, scatter diagrams, hypothesis testing.\n819: \n820: **4. I — Improve:**\n821: - Develop and implement solutions to eliminate root causes.\n822: - Design experiments to test proposed solutions.\n823: - Optimize the process.\n824: - Tools: Brainstorming, design of experiments (DOE), pilot testing.\n825: \n826: **5. C — Control:**\n827: - Control the improved process to sustain the gains.\n828: - Implement control charts and monitoring systems.\n829: - Standardize the new process and document procedures.\n830: - Tools: Control charts, SOPs, training programs.\n831: \n832: ### Advantages of Six Sigma:\n833: 1. Reduces defects significantly (3.4 DPMO).\n834: 2. Improves customer satisfaction.\n835: 3. Reduces cost of poor quality (rework, scrap, warranty).\n836: 4. Data-driven decision making — eliminates guesswork.\n837: 5. Creates a culture of continuous improvement.\n838: \n839: ### Limitations of Six Sigma:\n840: 1. Requires significant investment in training (Green Belt, Black Belt).\n841: 2. Time-consuming — projects typically take 3-6 months.\n842: 3. Not suitable for small organizations with limited resources.\n843: 4. Over-reliance on data — may miss creative solutions.\n844: 5. Resistance to change from employees.\n845: \n846: ---\n847: \n848: ## Q23. Explain the concept and objectives of Quality Circles. [4 Marks]\n849: \n850: ### Definition:\n851: \n852: A Quality Circle (QC) is a small group of **6-12 workers** from the same work area who **voluntarily** meet regularly (usually once a week for about an hour) to identify, analyze, and solve work-related quality problems and present solutions to management.\n853: \n854: **Originated in:** Japan (1962), introduced by Dr. Kaoru Ishikawa.\n855: \n856: ### Concept:\n857: - Workers who do the job daily know the problems best.\n858: - Voluntary participation — no one is forced to join.\n859: - The group is trained in problem-solving tools (Pareto chart, cause-and-effect diagram, brainstorming).\n860: - Solutions are presented to management for approval and implementation.\n861: - Focus is on quality improvement, cost reduction, safety, and productivity.\n862: \n863: ### Objectives of Quality Circles:\n864: \n865: 1. **Improve quality** — Identify and eliminate quality problems at the source.\n866: 2. **Reduce errors and defects** — Workers suggest practical solutions to recurring defects.\n867: 3. **Improve productivity** — Find ways to work more efficiently and eliminate waste.\n868: 4. **Reduce cost** — Lower scrap, rework, and material wastage.\n869: 5. **Develop people** — Enhance skills and knowledge of workers through training and participation.\n870: 6. **Boost morale** — Give workers a sense of ownership and involvement in decision-making.\n871: 7. **Improve communication** — Better understanding between workers and management.\n872: 8. **Create a quality culture** — Build awareness of quality among all workers.\n873: 9. **Promote teamwork** — Workers learn to work together as a team.\n874: 10. **Safety improvement** — Identify and eliminate potential safety hazards.\n875: \n876: ### Structure of Quality Circle:\n877: \n878: - **Members** — 6-12 workers from the same work area\n879: - **Leader** — Usually the supervisor of the group\n880: - **Facilitator** — A senior person who provides guidance and training\n881: - **Steering Committee** — Management team that reviews and approves proposals\n882: \n883: ---\n884: \n885: ## Q24. What is Total Quality Management (TQM)? Enlist its core principles. [4-7 Marks]\n886: \n887: ### Definition:\n888: \n889: Total Quality Management (TQM) is a management philosophy and approach that focuses on **continuous improvement of quality** in all aspects of an organization's operations. It involves **all employees** — from top management to the shop floor worker — working together to improve products, services, processes, and the culture of the organization.\n890: \n891: **Key Words:** Total (everyone), Quality (meeting customer requirements), Management (systematic approach).\n892: \n893: ### Core Principles of TQM:\n894: \n895: **1. Customer Focus:**\n896: - The customer (internal and external) defines quality.\n897: - All activities should aim to meet or exceed customer expectations.\n898: \n899: **2. Total Employee Involvement:**\n900: - Every employee at every level is responsible for quality.\n901: - Empowerment, training, and teamwork are essential.\n902: \n903: **3. Continuous Improvement (Kaizen):**\n904: - Quality is not a destination but a continuous journey.\n905: - Use PDCA cycle (Plan → Do → Check → Act) for ongoing improvement.\n906: \n907: **4. Process-Centered Approach:**\n908: - Focus on improving processes rather than blaming individuals.\n909: - Well-defined processes lead to consistent quality.\n910: \n911: **5. Fact-Based Decision Making:**\n912: - Decisions should be based on data and statistical analysis, not gut feelings.\n913: - Use SQC tools, Pareto charts, control charts for analysis.\n914: \n915: **6. Integrated System:**\n916: - All departments and functions must work together as a unified system.\n917: - Break down departmental barriers.\n918: \n919: **7. Strategic and Systematic Approach:**\n920: - Quality improvement must be planned strategically — aligned with the organization's vision and mission.\n921: \n922: **8. Communication:**\n923: - Open and transparent communication at all levels — top-down, bottom-up, and horizontal.\n924: \n925: **9. Supplier Partnership:**\n926: - Build long-term partnerships with suppliers based on trust and mutual benefit.\n927: - Supplier quality directly affects product quality.\n928: \n929: ### Benefits of TQM:\n930: 1. Improved customer satisfaction and loyalty.\n931: 2. Reduced costs through fewer defects, less rework and scrap.\n932: 3. Improved employee morale and involvement.\n933: 4. Competitive advantage in the market.\n934: 5. Higher productivity and profitability.\n935: \n936: ---\n937: \n938: ## Q25. Write a short note comparing ISO 9000, ISO 14000, and QS 9000. [4-7 Marks]\n939: \n940: ### ISO 9000 — Quality Management System:\n941: \n942: - **Full Form:** International Organization for Standardization — 9000 series.\n943: - **Purpose:** Provides guidelines and requirements for establishing a Quality Management System (QMS).\n944: - **Focus:** Ensuring consistent quality of products and services to meet customer requirements.\n945: - **Key Standard:** ISO 9001 — Requirements for QMS (certifiable standard).\n946: - **Scope:** Applicable to ANY organization — manufacturing, service, government, education.\n947: - **Principles:** Customer focus, leadership, process approach, continuous improvement, evidence-based decision making.\n948: - **Certification:** Third-party audit and certification by accredited bodies.\n949: \n950: ### ISO 14000 — Environmental Management System:\n951: \n952: - **Full Form:** International Organization for Standardization — 14000 series.\n953: - **Purpose:** Provides guidelines for establishing an Environmental Management System (EMS).\n954: - **Focus:** Minimizing the harmful effects of an organization's activities on the environment.\n955: - **Key Standard:** ISO 14001 — Requirements for EMS (certifiable standard).\n956: - **Scope:** Applicable to ANY organization wanting to improve environmental performance.\n957: - **Areas Covered:** Air pollution, water pollution, waste management, energy conservation, resource utilization, compliance with environmental laws.\n958: - **Goal:** Sustainable development and environmental protection.\n959: \n960: ### QS 9000 — Automotive Quality Standard:\n961: \n962: - **Full Form:** Quality System 9000.\n963: - **Purpose:** Quality management standard specifically developed for the **automotive industry**.\n964: - **Developed by:** The \"Big Three\" American automobile manufacturers — General Motors, Ford, and Chrysler.\n965: - **Focus:** Ensuring quality requirements of automotive suppliers and sub-suppliers.\n966: - **Based on:** ISO 9001 + additional automotive-specific requirements.\n967: - **Additional Requirements:** Production Part Approval Process (PPAP), Continuous Improvement, Manufacturing Capabilities.\n968: - **Note:** QS 9000 has been largely replaced by **IATF 16949** (current automotive quality standard), but GTU still asks about QS 9000.\n969: \n970: ### Comparison Table:\n971: \n972: | Criteria | ISO 9000 | ISO 14000 | QS 9000 |\n973: |----------|----------|-----------|---------|\n974: | Focus | Quality Management | Environmental Management | Automotive Quality |\n975: | Applicability | All industries | All industries | Automotive industry only |\n976: | Objective | Customer satisfaction | Environmental protection | Automotive supplier quality |\n977: | Key Standard | ISO 9001 | ISO 14001 | QS 9000 (now IATF 16949) |\n978: | Certification | Yes (ISO 9001) | Yes (ISO 14001) | Yes |\n979: | Developed by | ISO (Geneva) | ISO (Geneva) | GM, Ford, Chrysler |\n980: | Year | 1987 (first published) | 1996 | 1994 |\n981: \n982: ---\n983: ---\n984: \n985: # DIAGRAM PROMPTS\n986: \n987: Below are the diagrams you need. Use these prompts to generate clean exam-style diagrams.\n988: \n989: ### Diagram 1: Product Layout vs Process Layout\n990: **Prompt:** \"Draw a clean engineering diagram showing two factory layouts side by side. LEFT side labeled 'Product Layout (Line Layout)' showing machines arranged in a straight line sequence: Raw Material → Lathe → Milling → Drilling → Grinding → Assembly → Finished Product, with arrows showing one-directional flow. RIGHT side labeled 'Process Layout (Functional Layout)' showing grouped departments: Lathe Section (3 lathes), Milling Section (3 milling machines), Drilling Section (3 drills), Grinding Section (3 grinders), with zigzag arrows showing material moving between departments. Black and white, clean lines, engineering textbook style, labeled clearly.\"\n991: \n992: ### Diagram 2: Fixed Position Layout\n993: **Prompt:** \"Draw a clean engineering diagram of Fixed Position Layout. Show a large central product (labeled 'SHIP / AIRCRAFT' or 'MAIN PRODUCT') in the center. Around it show workers, machines, tools, and materials coming TO the product from all sides with arrows pointing inward. Show labels: 'Workers', 'Machines brought to site', 'Raw Materials', 'Tools & Equipment', 'Sub-assemblies' all pointing toward center. Black and white, textbook style.\"\n994: \n995: ### Diagram 3: Operation Process Chart (Bolt Manufacturing)\n996: **Prompt:** \"Draw a clean Operation Process Chart (OPC) for manufacturing a bolt. Show a vertical line from top to bottom. On this line, draw alternating circles (operations) and squares (inspections) in sequence: O-1 Cut rod, I-1 Check length, O-2 Turn on lathe, I-2 Check diameter, O-3 Thread cutting, I-3 Inspect thread, O-4 Head forging, O-5 Heat treatment, O-6 Surface finishing, I-4 Final inspection. Label 'Steel Rod' entering from top with horizontal line. Include a summary box showing Operations=6, Inspections=4. Black and white, clean engineering chart style.\"\n997: \n998: ### Diagram 4: Two-Handed Process Chart\n999: **Prompt:** \"Draw a Two-Handed Process Chart (Left Hand Right Hand chart) for assembling a nut and bolt. Show two columns — LEFT HAND and RIGHT HAND — with activities listed side by side. Use symbols: circle for operation, arrow for transport, triangle for hold, D for delay. Show 8 steps for each hand. Include a summary table at bottom counting operations, transports, delays, and holds for each hand. Clean black and white, engineering textbook format.\"\n1000: \n1001: ### Diagram 5: Flow Process Chart Symbols\n1002: **Prompt:** \"Draw the 5 standard ASME process chart symbols in a clean vertical list. 1) Large circle = OPERATION, 2) Arrow = TRANSPORT, 3) Square = INSPECTION, 4) Capital letter D = DELAY, 5) Inverted triangle (equilateral triangle pointing down) = STORAGE. Also show a combined symbol: circle inside a square = COMBINED ACTIVITY (Operation + Inspection). Each symbol drawn clearly with its name and brief definition next to it. Black and white, engineering reference card style.\"\n1003: \n1004: ### Diagram 6: DMAIC Cycle (Six Sigma)\n1005: **Prompt:** \"Draw a circular diagram showing the DMAIC cycle of Six Sigma. Five segments in a circle or pentagon: DEFINE → MEASURE → ANALYZE → IMPROVE → CONTROL, connected by arrows in clockwise direction. Each segment should have 2-3 bullet points: Define (problem, goals, CTQ), Measure (data collection, baseline), Analyze (root cause, fishbone), Improve (solutions, pilot), Control (monitor, sustain). Clean, black and white, textbook style.\"\n1006: \n1007: ### Diagram 7: Quality Circle Structure\n1008: **Prompt:** \"Draw an organizational chart showing the structure of a Quality Circle. At top: Steering Committee (management). Below it: Facilitator/Coordinator. Below that: Quality Circle Leader (supervisor). At bottom: 6-12 circle members (workers) shown as small icons in a circle formation meeting around a table. Show arrows indicating flow of ideas upward and decisions downward. Clean black and white, simple organizational chart style.\"\n1009: \n1010: ### Diagram 8: Normal Work Area and Maximum Work Area\n1011: **Prompt:** \"Draw a top-view diagram of a worker sitting at a workstation, showing two concentric semicircular arcs. Inner arc labeled 'Normal Work Area' (radius ~40-45 cm, reached by forearm pivot at elbow). Outer arc labeled 'Maximum Work Area' (radius ~55-60 cm, reached by full arm extension from shoulder). Show left hand and right hand arcs overlapping in front. Tools and materials should be placed within normal work area. Clean black and white, ergonomics textbook style.\"\n1012: \n1013: ---\n1014: ---\n1015: \n1016: # VERIFICATION CHECKLIST\n1017: \n1018: | # | Question | Chapter | Marks | Tier | Covered? |\n1019: |---|----------|---------|:-----:|:----:|:--------:|\n1020: | 1 | Types of plant layout + Product vs Process differentiation | Ch 1 | 7 | 🔴 T1 | ✅ |\n1021: | 2 | Objectives of good plant layout | Ch 1 | 3 | 🟢 T3 | ✅ |\n1022: | 3 | Fixed position layout | Ch 1 | 3 | 🟢 T3 | ✅ |\n1023: | 4 | Factors for plant location + importance | Ch 1 | 4-7 | 🔴 T1 | ✅ |\n1024: | 5 | Site selection — Thermal/Nuclear/Cement | Ch 1 | 7 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1025: | 6 | Definition of productivity + ways to improve | Ch 2 | 7 | 🔴 T1 | ✅ |\n1026: | 7 | Factors affecting productivity | Ch 2 | 7 | 🔴 T1 | ✅ |\n1027: | 8 | Reasons for increase and decrease in productivity | Ch 2 | 7 | 🔴 T1 | ✅ |\n1028: | 9 | Tools and techniques to improve productivity | Ch 2 | 7 | 🔴 T1 | ✅ |\n1029: | 10 | Principles of motion economy + workplace design | Ch 2 | 7 | 🔴 T1 | ✅ |\n1030: | 11 | Method study — definition + objectives | Ch 2 | 3-4 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1031: | 12 | Basic procedure of method study | Ch 2 | 3 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1032: | 13 | Criteria for selecting job for method study | Ch 2 | 3 | 🟢 T3 | ✅ |\n1033: | 14 | Operation Process Chart with example | Ch 2 | 4-7 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1034: | 15 | Two-Handed Process Chart with sketch | Ch 2 | 4-7 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1035: | 16 | Process chart symbols — meanings | Ch 2 | 4 | 🟢 T3 | ✅ |\n1036: | 17 | Need and objective of Industrial Legislation | Ch 3 | 3-4 | 🔴 T1 | ✅ |\n1037: | 18 | Factories Act 1948 — Health, Safety, Working Hours | Ch 3 | 7 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1038: | 19 | Industrial Disputes Act 1947 — objectives | Ch 3 | 4 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1039: | 20 | Inspection — functions, types, objectives | Ch 4 | 7 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1040: | 21 | QC vs QA + Quality Audit | Ch 4 | 4 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1041: | 22 | Six Sigma — DMAIC principle and methodology | Ch 4 | 4-7 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1042: | 23 | Quality Circles — concept, objectives | Ch 4 | 4 | 🟡 T2 | ✅ |\n1043: | 24 | TQM — concept, core principles | Ch 4 | 4-7 | 🟢 T3 | ✅ |\n1044: | 25 | ISO 9000 / 14000 / QS 9000 comparison | Ch 4 | 4-7 | 🟢 T3 | ✅ |\n1045: \n1046: **EXCLUDED (as requested):**\n1047: - ~~Therbligs — list, symbols, importance~~ (4 questions skipped)\n1048: - ~~String Diagram — explanation & advantages~~ (1 question skipped)\n1049: \n1050: **Total questions answered: 25 ✅ | Skipped: 5**\n1051: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":11,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T07:48:16Z","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Specificity**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool selection, always aiming for the most precise one available. Efficiency hinges on using specialized tools whenever possible, steering clear of more general-purpose options unless absolutely necessary. The goal is to minimize complexity and maximize effectiveness in every task.\n\n\n**Analyzing Tool Choices**\n\nI've been reviewing the recent tool options, making sure I avoid `cat` for file creation and `grep` within bash unless critical. I'm prioritizing `grep_search` and aiming for specific tools. Considering the 1051-line file, I'm leaning toward a Python script for processing and outputting to the scratch pad, so that I can then use `cat` (read) to get the data, but I'm exploring alternatives to minimize tool use.\n\n\n**Refining Processing Approach**\n\nI'm now revising my strategy based on the prompt's emphasis on using multiple agents and checking for accuracy. This means crafting a Python script, specifically `scratch/processor.py`, to parse the file, apply the formatting rules I've outlined, and write the output to a `.txt` file, which I'll then read to `echo`. The core focus is on question-by-question formatting with single spacing, no punctuation (except `/`), hyphen replacement with spaces, and number preservation, all to facilitate multi-agent use as per the user's instructions.\n\n\n**Adjusting Formatting Logic**\n\nI'm now refining the Python script's logic to handle the specific formatting demands. The goal is to bold headings using `**`, and remove all listed punctuation. The approach involves reading the entire file, splitting it by `## Q` (question delimiters), processing each question block individually. I'm exploring how to best handle potential variations in section dividers and question structures, focusing on making headings bold.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python -c \"\nimport re\n\ndef process_text(text):\n    # Split the text by '## Q' to identify questions, but wait, there are also chapters.\n    # Let's split by lines and accumulate.\n    \n    # We want to format each logical block (like a question) as a single line.\n    # Let's define the logical blocks:\n    # A block starts with '## Q' or '# ' or '### Diagram' or '---' etc.\n    # Actually, the user says \\\"when question get over start with new line\\\"\n    # Let's just split by '## Q' and process each.\n    \n    with open(r'C:\\Users\\renu5\\Downloads\\college\\College sem 5\\bie artifacts\\BIE_complete_answer_bank.md', 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        content = f.read()\n    \n    # Split by '## Q'\n    parts = re.split(r'(?=## Q\\d+)', content)\n    \n    output_lines = []\n    for part in parts:\n        if not part.strip():\n            continue\n            \n        # Process this part\n        # 1. Identify headings and bold them. Markdown headings: #, ##, ###, or **text**\n        # We'll just keep the text inside ** ** as bold.\n        # For # Headings, we convert them to bold.\n        part = re.sub(r'#+\\s*(.+)', r'**\\1**', part)\n        \n        # 2. Extract bold items so we don't mess them up, or just process character by character.\n        # Actually, let's just remove punctuation from the whole string.\n        # Allowed chars: alphanumeric, spaces, •, 1-9, ---, /\n        # We need to keep ** for bolding. So * is allowed if it's for bolding.\n        # Wait, the prompt says \\\"NO PUNCTUATION: DO NOT use any standard punctuation... The only non-alphanumeric characters allowed are the numbers, the bullet symbol (•), the dash divider (---), and forward slashes (///////).\\\"\n        # If I keep **, I am keeping asterisks, which is punctuation?\n        # But the prompt also says \\\"Headings: Make all main topics and subtopics bold (e.g., Main Topic).\\\"\n        # In markdown, bold is done via ** or __ or UI rich text. I will use ** as in my previous turn.\n        \n        # Replace --- with a special token so it doesn't get removed.\n        part = part.replace('---', ' _DASHES_ ')\n        \n        # Replace ** with a special token\n        part = part.replace('**', ' _BOLD_ ')\n        \n        # We want to keep •, /, alphanumeric, and spaces.\n        # Let's remove hyphen/em-dash/en-dash unless they were part of ---\n        # Replace hyphens with spaces\n        part = part.replace('-', ' ')\n        part = part.replace('—', ' ')\n        part = part.replace('_', ' ') # Wait, we just added _DASHES_\n        \n        # Restore our tokens safely later. Let's do char by char or regex.\n        # Allowed characters: a-z, A-Z, 0-9, \\s, •, /\n        # We want to keep _DASHES_ and _BOLD_ intact.\n        \n        # Let's use a simpler approach.\n        # First, bold all # headings.\n        lines = part.split('\\n')\n        new_lines = []\n        for line in lines:\n            line = line.strip()\n            if not line:\n                continue\n            if line.startswith('**') and line.endswith('**'):\n                # Already bold\n                pass\n            elif re.match(r'^#+\\s+', line):\n                line = '**' + re.sub(r'^#+\\s+', '', line) + '**'\n            # Also table rows? Tables have |, -, let's just process them.\n            new_lines.append(line)\n        \n        text = ' '.join(new_lines)\n        \n        # Now remove all punctuation except allowed ones.\n        # Allowed: alphanumeric, whitespace, •, /, *, -\n        # Wait, we only want to keep * if it's part of **.\n        # Let's temporarily hide ** and ---\n        text = text.replace('**', 'B_O_L_D')\n        text = text.replace('---', 'D_A_S_H_E_S')\n        \n        # Remove all other punctuation\n        # [^\\w\\s•/B_O_L_D_A_S_H_E_S] -> wait, \\w includes underscore.\n        \n        res = []\n        for char in text:\n            if char.isalnum() or char.isspace() or char in ['•', '/']:\n                res.append(char)\n            elif char in ['B', '_', 'O', 'L', 'D', 'A', 'S', 'H', 'E']:\n                # This is safe because these are alphanumeric and underscore, which we handle.\n                # Actually, let's just do regex substitution.\n                res.append(char) # Since they are alphanumeric + underscore\n            else:\n                res.append(' ')\n        \n        text = ''.join(res)\n        \n        # Remove punctuation by explicitly regexing out anything not alphanumeric or space or • or /\n        import string\n        allowed = set(string.ascii_letters + string.digits + ' \\t\\n\\r•/')\n        text = ''.join(c if c in allowed or c == '_' else ' ' for c in text)\n        \n        # Restore ** and ---\n        text = text.replace('B_O_L_D', '**')\n        text = text.replace('D_A_S_H_E_S', '---')\n        \n        # Clean up multiple spaces\n        text = re.sub(r'\\s+', ' ', text).strip()\n        \n        output_lines.append(text)\n\n    # Join with newlines\n    final_output = '\\n'.join(output_lines)\n    \n    with open('C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/formatted_bank.md', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        f.write(final_output)\n    print('Processing complete. Output written to scratch.')\n\"","Cwd":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch","IsDaemon":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Running formatting script","toolSummary":"Run python formatting script"}}]}
